.TH KAGI-VOLUME-NBD 1 "November 2026" "Kagi 0.39.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kagi-volume-nbd \- serve a sparse Kagi volume over NBD
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kagi-volume-nbd \-\-api
.I URL
.B \-\-volume
.I ID
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Exposes one existing Kagi volume using the fixed-newstyle NBD handshake.
Reads, writes, and trim requests use the volume range and unmap APIs.
Unmapping complete extents can release sparse backing storage.
The frontend acknowledges flush locally.
The backend's write response defines when a write completes.
.PP
Each API request carries the configured initiator identifier so the volume
service can enforce its reservation policy.
NBD carries block operations.
For SCSI persistent-reservation CDBs, use a frontend that explicitly maps
those commands to Kagi's reservation API.
.PP
The NBD listener uses plain TCP and accepts no client credentials.
Keep it on loopback or an isolated storage network.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-api " URL"
Base URL of the Kagi volume API.
.TP
.BI \-\-volume " ID"
ID of an existing Kagi volume.
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " ADDRESS:PORT"
Local NBD listen address.
The default is
.BR 127.0.0.1:10809 .
.TP
.BI \-\-initiator " ID"
Stable initiator to pass to the Kagi volume API.
The default is
.BR nbd:anonymous .
.TP
.B \-\-read-only
Advertise the export as read-only and reject write and trim requests.
A read-only backend volume also makes the export read-only.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
kagi-volume-nbd \-\-api http://127.0.0.1:7400 \-\-volume demo \e
    \-\-initiator lab:vm1
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kagi-cluster-host (1),
.BR kagi-volume-attach (1),
.BR kagi-volume-qemu-scsi (1),
.BR kagi-volume-libvirt-scsi (1),
.BR kagi-volume-iscsi-export (1)
